    "analysis": "<p>React Native's new architecture, powered by the JavaScript Interface (JSI), introduces a direct bridge between JavaScript and native C++ code. A cornerstone of JSI is the `HostObject`, a C++ object that can expose properties and methods directly to the JavaScript runtime, allowing for high-performance synchronous calls. While powerful, this direct interaction opens the door to complex memory management challenges, specifically 'dangling references' and garbage collection (GC) desynchronization between the JavaScript engine and the native C++ runtime, particularly when using TurboModules.</p><p>A `HostObject` holds a pointer to an underlying native C++ object. The JavaScript engine's garbage collector manages the lifecycle of the `HostObject` on the JS side. However, the native C++ object's lifecycle is managed by C++ memory rules (e.g., `new`/`delete`, `std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`). If the native C++ object is deallocated while the JavaScript `HostObject` still holds a reference (a 'dangling reference'), any subsequent access from JavaScript to that `HostObject` will attempt to dereference invalid memory, leading to crashes, undefined behavior, or security vulnerabilities. Conversely, if the C++ object holds a strong reference to the JavaScript `HostObject` and prevents its garbage collection, it can lead to memory leaks. This desynchronization often arises because the JavaScript GC operates asynchronously and non-deterministically, making it hard to predict when a `HostObject` will be collected, and thus, when its native counterpart can be safely deallocated.</p>",
    "root_cause": "The native C++ object backing a JSI `HostObject` is deallocated prematurely before all JavaScript references to the `HostObject` are released, or the native object holds an improper strong reference to the JS object. This causes a desync between native C++ memory management and JavaScript's garbage collection, resulting in dangling pointers, use-after-free errors, or memory leaks.",
    "bad_code": "```cpp\n// Bad example: Native object lifetime not tied to JSI HostObject\nclass NativeManager {\npublic:\n    std::string data = \"Sensitive Data\";\n    // ... other resources\n};\n\nclass JSIHostObjectBad : public facebook::jsi::HostObject {\npublic:\n    JSIHostObjectBad(std::shared_ptr<NativeManager> manager) : manager_(manager) {}\n\n    facebook::jsi::Value get(facebook::jsi::Runtime& runtime, const facebook::jsi::PropNameID& name) override {\n        auto propName = name.utf8(runtime);\n        if (propName == \"getData\") {\n            return facebook::jsi::Function::createFromHostFunction(\n                runtime, name, 0, [this](facebook::jsi::Runtime& runtime, const facebook::jsi::Value&, const facebook::jsi::Value*,\n                                        size_t) -> facebook::jsi::Value {\n                    // If manager_ is already null/freed, this is use-after-free\n                    return facebook::jsi::String::createFromUtf8(runtime, manager_->data);\n                });\n        }\n        return facebook::jsi::Value::undefined();\n    }\n\nprivate:\n    // This shared_ptr might be the *only* strong reference preventing NativeManager destruction\n    // If another part of native code *also* manages NativeManager and frees it, \n    // this `manager_` could become dangling without the HostObject knowing.\n    std::shared_ptr<NativeManager> manager_;\n};\n\n// Problem: If NativeManager is managed externally and released, \n// but JSIHostObjectBad still exists in JS, manager_ becomes dangling.\n// The JSIHostObject itself doesn't directly manage the NativeManager's destruction.\n```",
    "solution_desc": "The robust solution for JSI `HostObject`s relies on establishing clear ownership and lifetime management. The most common and effective pattern involves using `std::shared_ptr` or `folly::Shared<T>` for the native C++ object, where the `HostObject` instance holds a strong `shared_ptr`. This ensures the native object persists as long as *any* `shared_ptr` to it exists, including the one held by the `HostObject`. When the JavaScript `HostObject` is garbage collected, its `shared_ptr` will be destroyed, decrementing the reference count. Once the count reaches zero, the native object is safely deallocated. For situations where the native object needs to outlive the JavaScript `HostObject` (e.g., a global singleton), the `HostObject` should hold a `std::weak_ptr` and check for validity before use. Additionally, ensure that native C++ callbacks to JavaScript pass `jsi::Value`s (e.g., `jsi::Function`s) owned by `jsi::Object::get(runtime, \"myCallback\").asObject(runtime).asFunction(runtime)` into an `jsi::Persistent` to prevent their premature collection. Explicitly managing the lifecycle of native objects and carefully considering reference counting is paramount.",
    "good_code": "```cpp\n// Good example: Native object lifetime managed by shared_ptr\nclass NativeManager {\npublic:\n    std::string data = \"Sensitive Data\";\n    // ... other resources\n    ~NativeManager() { /* Clean up resources */ }\n};\n\nclass JSIHostObjectGood : public facebook::jsi::HostObject {\npublic:\n    // JSIHostObjectGood now owns the NativeManager via shared_ptr\n    // NativeManager will be destructed when all shared_ptrs are gone.\n    JSIHostObjectGood(std::shared_ptr<NativeManager> manager) : manager_(manager) {}\n\n    facebook::jsi::Value get(facebook::jsi::Runtime& runtime, const facebook::jsi::PropNameID& name) override {\n        auto propName = name.utf8(runtime);\n        if (propName == \"getData\") {\n            // Capture 'this' (the shared_ptr) by value to extend its lifetime into the lambda\n            return facebook::jsi::Function::createFromHostFunction(\n                runtime, name, 0, [manager = manager_](facebook::jsi::Runtime& runtime, const facebook::jsi::Value&, \n                                        const facebook::jsi::Value*, size_t) -> facebook::jsi::Value {\n                    // 'manager' is a copy of the shared_ptr, ensuring NativeManager is alive \n                    // as long as this lambda (and thus the JS Function) can be called.\n                    return facebook::jsi::String::createFromUtf8(runtime, manager->data);\n                });\n        }\n        return facebook::jsi::Value::undefined();\n    }\n\nprivate:\n    std::shared_ptr<NativeManager> manager_;\n};\n\n// Usage in module initialization:\n// auto nativeManager = std::make_shared<NativeManager>();\n// runtime.global().setProperty(runtime, \"myJSIObject\", \n//    facebook::jsi::Object::createFromHostObject(runtime, std::make_shared<JSIHostObjectGood>(nativeManager)));\n```",
    "verification": "Verification involves rigorous testing and native debugging. Perform stress tests by frequently creating and destroying `HostObject` instances, especially within loops or under heavy concurrent usage, to trigger potential race conditions or GC desyncs. Monitor for native crashes (e.g., `EXC_BAD_ACCESS` on iOS, `SIGSEGV` on Android) using tools like Xcode's diagnostics, Android Studio's Logcat, or crash reporting services. Utilize native memory profiling tools (e.g., Instruments on iOS, LeakCanary on Android, or general purpose tools like Valgrind on Linux/macOS for C++ code) to detect memory leaks or use-after-free errors. Ensure that `~NativeManager()` destructors are called when expected, indicating proper resource cleanup. If possible, integrate unit tests that mock the JavaScript GC behavior to ensure native object lifetimes are correctly managed.",
